WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:07.304
NARRATOR: Begin by framing Process Model &
State as a question about inputs, decisions,

2
00:00:07.304 --> 00:00:08.859
and observable results.

3
00:00:08.859 --> 00:00:15.797
NARRATOR: A process combines an address space
with execution state and operating-system

4
00:00:15.797 --> 00:00:16.375
resources.

5
00:00:16.375 --> 00:00:23.803
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A running
web server process maintains its own stack,

6
00:00:23.803 --> 00:00:28.578
heap, and open file descriptors while
executing user-level code.

7
00:00:28.578 --> 00:00:35.719
NARRATOR: On screen, show a diagram of a
process with labeled components: address

8
00:00:35.719 --> 00:00:39.015
space, execution state, and resource
ownership.

9
00:00:39.015 --> 00:00:43.687
NARRATOR: Annotate each component with its
function in the OS model.

10
00:00:43.687 --> 00:00:49.959
NARRATOR: List the givens before choosing a
method, because an unstated assumption can

11
00:00:49.959 --> 00:00:51.406
change the conclusion.

12
00:00:51.406 --> 00:00:57.531
NARRATOR: This establishes the vocabulary and
scope needed for the remaining sections.

13
00:00:57.650 --> 00:01:04.590
NARRATOR: Now define System Calls & Privilege
Levels precisely enough that another learner

14
00:01:04.590 --> 00:01:06.725
could test the definition.

15
00:01:06.725 --> 00:01:12.850
NARRATOR: A system call transfers control from
user code to a privileged kernel service.

16
00:01:12.850 --> 00:01:19.566
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: When a
program requests disk I/O, it executes a

17
00:01:19.566 --> 00:01:25.803
system call instruction that switches
execution to the kernel to handle the request.

18
00:01:25.803 --> 00:01:32.374
NARRATOR: On screen, display a flowchart
showing user mode code calling a system call,

19
00:01:32.374 --> 00:01:38.006
transitioning to kernel mode, and returning to
user mode with a result.

20
00:01:38.006 --> 00:01:44.685
NARRATOR: Separate the formal definition from
an implementation detail, then identify what

21
00:01:44.685 --> 00:01:46.912
evidence would distinguish them.

22
00:01:46.912 --> 00:01:53.490
NARRATOR: A precise definition makes later
examples easier to evaluate and revise.

23
00:01:53.618 --> 00:01:59.889
NARRATOR: With the foundation in place,
examine how Traps & Processor State operates

24
00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:01.818
in a concrete sequence.

25
00:02:01.818 --> 00:02:08.470
NARRATOR: A trap records processor state so
the kernel can handle a system call,

26
00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:09.896
exception, or interrupt.

27
00:02:09.896 --> 00:02:17.250
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: When a
page fault occurs, the CPU traps the faulting

28
00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:23.624
instruction, saves the instruction pointer and
registers, and transfers control to the kernel

29
00:02:23.624 --> 00:02:24.114
handler.

30
00:02:24.114 --> 00:02:30.854
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a trap
sequence with arrows showing state capture,

31
00:02:30.854 --> 00:02:34.786
kernel entry, exception handling, and state
restoration.

32
00:02:34.786 --> 00:02:42.114
NARRATOR: Follow every arrow in order and name
the state before and after each transition.

33
00:02:42.114 --> 00:02:47.786
NARRATOR: The trace converts an abstract
statement into an inspectable sequence.

34
00:02:47.906 --> 00:02:55.182
NARRATOR: Next, compare the choices involved
in Page Tables & Virtual Memory and make the

35
00:02:55.182 --> 00:02:56.637
decision criteria explicit.

36
00:02:56.637 --> 00:03:03.027
NARRATOR: Page tables map virtual addresses to
physical memory with permission bits.

37
00:03:03.027 --> 00:03:09.043
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A page
table entry for a read-only code segment

38
00:03:09.043 --> 00:03:14.199
contains a valid bit, a present bit, and a
read-only permission flag.

39
00:03:14.199 --> 00:03:20.611
NARRATOR: On screen, show a page table
structure with columns for virtual address,

40
00:03:20.611 --> 00:03:25.543
physical address, and permission bits,
highlighting how mappings are resolved.

41
00:03:25.543 --> 00:03:32.256
NARRATOR: Compare alternatives against the
same constraints so the evaluation does not

42
00:03:32.256 --> 00:03:33.934
move the goalposts.

43
00:03:33.934 --> 00:03:40.777
NARRATOR: Using shared criteria turns a
preference into a defensible technical choice.

44
00:03:40.898 --> 00:03:48.154
NARRATOR: Apply Processor Scheduling to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

45
00:03:48.154 --> 00:03:49.191
isolated definition.

46
00:03:49.191 --> 00:03:55.426
NARRATOR: The scheduler selects runnable
processes and switches processor context.

47
00:03:55.426 --> 00:04:02.466
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: The OS
scheduler picks a ready process from the run

48
00:04:02.466 --> 00:04:08.098
queue, saves the current process's registers,
and loads the new process's context.

49
00:04:08.098 --> 00:04:15.474
NARRATOR: On screen, draw a scheduler loop
with states: ready queue, running, blocked,

50
00:04:15.474 --> 00:04:18.879
and context switch arrows between processes.

51
00:04:18.879 --> 00:04:25.300
NARRATOR: Work from the concrete inputs to the
result, checking units, types, or invariants

52
00:04:25.300 --> 00:04:26.676
at each step.

53
00:04:26.676 --> 00:04:31.785
NARRATOR: The worked case shows both how to
apply the method and how to check it.

54
00:04:31.898 --> 00:04:38.470
NARRATOR: Examine the tradeoffs in Locks &
Synchronization, including what improves and

55
00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:40.660
what becomes more difficult.

56
00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:46.941
NARRATOR: Locks protect invariants when
concurrent execution can access shared state.

57
00:04:46.941 --> 00:04:54.650
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A mutex
lock ensures that only one thread can modify a

58
00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:58.504
shared counter at a time, preventing race
conditions.

59
00:04:58.504 --> 00:05:04.945
NARRATOR: On screen, show a lock
acquisition/release sequence with two

60
00:05:04.945 --> 00:05:10.097
concurrent threads, highlighting mutual
exclusion and invariant protection.

61
00:05:10.097 --> 00:05:16.558
NARRATOR: State the benefit, the cost, and the
condition under which the tradeoff is

62
00:05:16.558 --> 00:05:17.019
acceptable.

63
00:05:17.019 --> 00:05:24.519
NARRATOR: Making the tradeoff visible prevents
a local improvement from hiding a larger cost.

64
00:05:24.519 --> 00:05:31.468
NARRATOR: Name each dependency explicitly, and
keep labels and representations consistent

65
00:05:31.468 --> 00:05:33.363
throughout the analysis.

66
00:05:33.482 --> 00:05:39.602
NARRATOR: Stress-test the reasoning around
Deadlock & Resource Management with an edge

67
00:05:39.602 --> 00:05:43.682
case and identify which assumption controls
the outcome.

68
00:05:43.682 --> 00:05:51.669
NARRATOR: Analysis distinguishes user mode,
privileged kernel code, hardware translation,

69
00:05:51.669 --> 00:05:57.260
interrupt state, resource ownership, and
synchronization invariants.

70
00:05:57.260 --> 00:06:03.988
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Two
processes each hold one file descriptor and

71
00:06:03.988 --> 00:06:09.681
wait for the other's, creating a circular
dependency that halts progress.

72
00:06:09.681 --> 00:06:17.278
NARRATOR: On screen, illustrate a deadlock
scenario with two processes and two resources,

73
00:06:17.278 --> 00:06:21.369
showing circular wait arrows and resource
ownership.

74
00:06:21.369 --> 00:06:27.487
NARRATOR: Change one assumption at a time and
observe which part of the reasoning no longer

75
00:06:27.487 --> 00:06:27.869
holds.

76
00:06:27.869 --> 00:06:34.186
NARRATOR: The edge case reveals the boundary
of the claim without discarding the useful

77
00:06:34.186 --> 00:06:35.088
core idea.

78
00:06:35.210 --> 00:06:41.692
NARRATOR: Use Multiprogramming &
Multiprocessing to diagnose a failure by

79
00:06:41.692 --> 00:06:45.581
separating symptoms, causes, and corrective
action.

80
00:06:45.581 --> 00:06:51.055
NARRATOR: Topics include process
synchronization, interprocess communication,

81
00:06:51.055 --> 00:06:56.530
deadlock, multiprogramming and
multiprocessing, processor scheduling and

82
00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:05.132
resource management, memory management, static
and dynamic relocation, virtual memory, file

83
00:07:05.132 --> 00:07:13.735
systems, logical and physical I/O, device
allocation, I/O processor scheduling, process

84
00:07:13.735 --> 00:07:16.081
and resource protection.

85
00:07:16.081 --> 00:07:22.048
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A server
handles multiple client requests

86
00:07:22.048 --> 00:07:27.472
simultaneously by switching between processes
on a single CPU core.

87
00:07:27.472 --> 00:07:34.796
NARRATOR: On screen, show a CPU core with
multiple process contexts interleaved,

88
00:07:34.796 --> 00:07:37.847
emphasizing time-sharing and resource
allocation.

89
00:07:37.847 --> 00:07:43.624
NARRATOR: Locate the first point where
observed behavior diverges from expected

90
00:07:43.624 --> 00:07:47.300
behavior, then test the smallest plausible
cause.

91
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:53.225
NARRATOR: The diagnosis is strongest when
another person can reproduce the observation

92
00:07:53.225 --> 00:07:54.706
and the test.

93
00:07:54.818 --> 00:08:01.263
NARRATOR: Connect File Systems & I/O to the
earlier ideas and explain how the pieces work

94
00:08:01.263 --> 00:08:02.471
as one system.

95
00:08:02.471 --> 00:08:09.408
NARRATOR: The file system organizes persistent
data through inodes, directories, and logged

96
00:08:09.408 --> 00:08:09.986
updates.

97
00:08:09.986 --> 00:08:16.844
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: A
directory entry points to an inode that stores

98
00:08:16.844 --> 00:08:21.252
file metadata, permissions, and block
addresses for data storage.

99
00:08:21.252 --> 00:08:28.796
NARRATOR: On screen, display a file system
tree with directories, inodes, and block

100
00:08:28.796 --> 00:08:33.439
clusters, showing how data is organized and
updated.

101
00:08:33.439 --> 00:08:42.439
NARRATOR: Explain each connection explicitly
and verify that information retains the same

102
00:08:42.439 --> 00:08:44.689
meaning across representations.

103
00:08:44.689 --> 00:08:51.537
NARRATOR: The synthesis matters only if every
connection remains supported by the original

104
00:08:51.537 --> 00:08:52.064
evidence.

105
00:08:52.178 --> 00:08:57.789
NARRATOR: Finish with Practical Analysis
Checklist as a practical checklist that can

106
00:08:57.789 --> 00:08:59.659
guide a new analysis.

107
00:08:59.659 --> 00:09:06.434
NARRATOR: Architecture analysis preserves data
dependencies, control dependencies, and

108
00:09:06.434 --> 00:09:07.940
architectural state.

109
00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:14.326
NARRATOR: Consider this worked case: Audit a
kernel module to ensure all shared variables

110
00:09:14.326 --> 00:09:19.799
are protected by locks and that page table
permissions match access rights.

111
00:09:19.799 --> 00:09:27.981
NARRATOR: On screen, provide a checklist
graphic with checkboxes for each invariant:

112
00:09:27.981 --> 00:09:34.117
user/kernel mode, hardware translation,
interrupt handling, resource ownership, and

113
00:09:34.117 --> 00:09:34.799
synchronization.

114
00:09:34.799 --> 00:09:42.518
NARRATOR: For each checklist item, record the
evidence, the remaining uncertainty, and the

115
00:09:42.518 --> 00:09:44.299
next verification step.

116
00:09:44.299 --> 00:09:50.291
NARRATOR: The result is a repeatable
procedure, not a conclusion that depends on

117
00:09:50.291 --> 00:09:51.674
memory or intuition.
